     ggeennaassssyymm..sshh — emit an assym.h file

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     sshh ggeennaassssyymm..sshh [--cc] _C _c_o_m_p_i_l_e_r _i_n_v_o_c_a_t_i_o_n

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     ggeennaassssyymm..sshh is a shell script normally used during the kernel build
     process to create an assym.h file.  This file defines a number of cpp
     constants derived from the configuration information ggeennaassssyymm..sshh reads
     from stdin.  The generated file is used by kernel sources written in as‐
     sembler to gain access to information (e.g. structure offsets and sizes)
     normally only known to the C compiler.

     ggeennaassssyymm..sshh resides in the _/_s_y_s_/_k_e_r_n directory.  Arguments to ggeennaassssyymm..sshh
     are usually of the form _$_{_C_C_} _$_{_C_F_L_A_G_S_} _$_{_C_P_P_F_L_A_G_S_} where _$_{_C_C_} is the C
     compiler used to compile the kernel, while _$_{_C_F_L_A_G_S_} and _$_{_C_P_P_F_L_A_G_S_} are
     flag arguments to the C compiler.  The script creates a C source file
     from its input.  Then the C compiler is called according to the script's
     arguments to compile this file.

     Normally ggeennaassssyymm..sshh instructs the C compiler to create an assembler
     source from the constructed C source.  The resulting file is then pro‐
     cessed to extract the information needed to create the assym.h file.  The
     --cc flag instructs ggeennaassssyymm..sshh to create slightly different code, generate
     an executable from this code and run it.  In both cases the assym.h file
     is written to stdout.

DDIIAAGGNNOOSSTTIICCSS
     Either self-explanatory, or generated by one of the programs called from
     the script.  The script will exit with the return code from the compiler
     or, in the --cc case, with the return code from the generated executable.

